    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a pilot formulation for a low-fat mayonnaise variant, but I'm running into some stability issues with the emulsion in high-acid environments (pH ~3.8). Could you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these acidic conditions, and how the charge on the emulsifier might be affecting the droplet coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In an acidic environment, the ester bonds in lecithin can become more reactive, altering its molecular structure and impacting its effectiveness at stabilizing the oil-water interface. The negative charge on lecithin facilitates electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged oil droplets and surrounding water molecules, preventing them from coalescing. However, any change in the molecular conformation of lecithin under high acidity that reduces this charge can directly influence the stability of the emulsion, making it more prone to droplet coalescence. To enhance stability, especially in low-pH, an alternative emulsifier or the addition of stabilizers that can withstand acidic environments, like synthetic E380, may be beneficial to maintain emulsion integrity.",
      "source": "target",
