    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Regarding the coalescence, if I'm seeing rapid phase separation despite the lecithin, could we be looking at a specific breakdown in the steric hindrance? I'm curious if the reduction in charge is causing the droplets to 'attack' each other and merge more aggressively than they would at a neutral pH. Is there a way to quantify this instability via the zeta potential?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rapid phase separation despite the presence of lecithin suggests challenges either in the formation of a stable emulsion or the breakdown of the steric and electrostatic barriers that prevent droplet coalescence. In low pH environments, acidic conditions could indeed lead to a breakdown in the steric hindrance provided by the oleic acid part of the lecithin molecule, allowing oil droplets to come closer together and merge, exacerbated by any reduction in surface charge that lessens the repulsive forces preventing coalescence.\n\nZeta potential measurements can indeed be used to quantify colloidal stability. It represents the electrical potential at the slipping plane of the emulsion particles and indicates their stability; higher values imply a higher barrier to coagulation. Zeta potential measurements would be especially useful in simulating the emulsion's stability over the pH scale relevant to high-acidity conditions. By monitoring zeta potential across this range, one can identify the pH at which the emulsion's stability begins to diminish, which would correspond to premature droplet coalescence. Additionally, consistency in the zeta potential across a range of conditions and concentrations could imply the robustness of the stabilizing shell around the oil droplets and its resilience against the destabilizing acidic environment.",
      "source": "target",
